<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand Trunk Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1852–1923</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grand Trunk Railway was a railway company incorporated in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1852 to link <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> and an ice-free Atlantic port. By 1860 its line ran from Sarnia through Montreal to Portland, Maine, crossing the St Lawrence on the Victoria Bridge. Burdened by debt, it was taken over by the Canadian government after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and merged into Canadian National Railways in 1923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-railway.jpg" alt="Grand Trunk Railway" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Canadian National Railway Company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Trunk_Railway_System_Logo_(1896).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
